{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"puzzle-construction-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Puzzle construction track","description":"Takes one original puzzle from brief to finished page in gated steps, from audience and aha idea through construction and a blind testsolve to clean presentation, hints and an answer key.","category":"puzzles","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["design","build","verify"],"role":["individual","teacher","gamer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["topic","notes"],"output":["quiz","checklist","explanation"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["puzzle-construction","testsolving","aha-moment","hint-ladder","unique-solution"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["testsolve-puzzle","make-logic-puzzle","design-puzzle-hunt","write-riddles"]},"args":[{"name":"idea","description":"What you want to make and for whom, for example \"a cipher puzzle for my nephew's 12th birthday\" or \"a word puzzle for our office newsletter\". Rough ideas are fine.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"constraints","description":"Any limits, such as print only, no colour, ten minutes to solve, must give the answer TREASURE, or must work in Portuguese.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"concept","file":"steps/02-concept.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"construct","file":"steps/03-construct.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"testsolve","file":"steps/04-testsolve.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"finish","file":"steps/05-finish.md","stage":"build","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Builds one original puzzle from {{idea}}{{#constraints}} within these limits: {{constraints}}{{/constraints}}, one approved step at a time: a brief, a concept built around a single \"aha\", the construction with its full solution, a blind testsolve that hunts for alternate answers and unclued leaps, and the finished page with hints and an answer key. Puzzle editors work this way because a puzzle cannot be checked by the person who built it while they still remember the answer, so the testsolve step solves only from the solver-facing text. Each step stops for the constructor's approval or edits, and later steps build on the approved versions. If the constructor asks to skip the approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps in one reply and state each choice made at a skipped gate. Never present a puzzle whose solution has not been worked through in full, and never skip the blind uniqueness check, even when approvals are skipped.","steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Brief\n\nPin down who the puzzle is for and what it must do.\n\nIdea:\n{{idea}}\n{{#constraints}}Constraints: {{constraints}}{{/constraints}}\n\n1. If the idea does not say who will solve the puzzle, ask that one question and stop.\n2. Write a brief of six lines: solvers (age, experience, language), setting (print, screen, a room, a hunt), target solve time, difficulty, required answer or answer form, and hard limits (no colour, no internet, accessibility needs).\n3. Suggest two or three puzzle types that fit the brief (for example a logic grid, a cipher with an extraction, a word chain, a physical-prop puzzle), each with one line on why it fits and its main risk.\n\nStop and wait for the constructor to approve the brief and choose a type."},{"id":"concept","file":"steps/02-concept.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Concept\n\nFind the single idea that makes the puzzle satisfying.\n\n1. Offer three concepts for the chosen type. Each gives the \"aha\" (the insight the solver must have), how the solver first notices something is going on, and how they confirm they are right (a word that emerges, a checksum, a pattern that completes).\n2. For each, sketch the solve path in three to five steps and name the step most likely to stall solvers.\n3. Recommend one concept for the brief and say why.\n\nStop and wait for the constructor to choose or adjust a concept."},{"id":"construct","file":"steps/03-construct.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Construct\n\nBuild the puzzle and its full solution.\n\n1. Build the answer side first: the final answer, then every intermediate result, then the data the solver will see.\n2. Write the solver-facing text: title, short instructions only where needed, the puzzle data, and any flavour text. Flavour text may hint but must not mislead.\n3. Write the full intended solution as numbered steps, each step citing what in the puzzle justifies it.\n4. Check every fact, spelling, count and calculation in the data against the solution.\n5. Mark the solver-facing text and the solution clearly as separate blocks, so the next step can use only the first.\n\nStop and wait for the constructor to approve the draft or ask for changes."},{"id":"testsolve","file":"steps/04-testsolve.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Blind testsolve\n\nSolve the approved puzzle from its solver-facing text only, as a fresh solver would.\n\n1. Set the intended solution aside. Solve step by step from the solver-facing text, logging what you noticed, tried and guessed, and where you stalled.\n2. Compare your path with the intended solution and list every difference.\n3. Check for faults: an alternate answer that fits all the data; a step that needs a leap the puzzle does not clue; ambiguous wording; data errors; colour-only or other inaccessible information; no confirmation that the solver is on track.\n4. Estimate the real solve time and difficulty against the brief.\n5. Propose a specific fix for each fault, keeping the concept, and apply the fixes to a revised draft. If any fix changes the solve, re-solve the changed part.\n\nStop and wait for the constructor to approve the revised puzzle."},{"id":"finish","file":"steps/05-finish.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Finished page\n\nProduce the final materials.\n\n1. **Puzzle page:** the final solver-facing text, laid out for the setting in the brief (printable block, slide or card text).\n2. **Hints:** three graded hints, from where to look, to the method, to a partial result.\n3. **Answer key:** the answer and the full solve path in numbered steps.\n4. **Host notes:** setup, timing, the most likely stall point and what to say there, and any props.\n5. **Final checklist:** answer unique, every step clued, data checked, accessible, fits the target time."}]}
